WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.330
You were probably too busy dancing, jiving,
and having the time of your life during Mamma

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.040
Mia 2 to notice, but if you did try to make
sense of Mamma Mia's timeline, it might have

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.139
made your head spin!

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:15.900
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to explain five

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.840
crazy things about Mamma Mia's timeline which
make it all really rather confusing!

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.680
And I'll also be announcing the winners of
my Here We Go Again giveaway!

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.750
The wonderful world of Mamma Mia is not only
full of beautiful sparking blue seas, fabulously

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:34.470
fun spontaneous song-and-dance routines, and
opportunities to live rent-free if you happen

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.500
to help the right horse, but it also seems
that they've managed to perfect the art of

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.490
technological time travel!

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:45.010
We see Sophie and Bill's mother using some
pretty up-to-date iPhones and some rather

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:50.550
recent tablet models appear in Harry's Tokyo
meeting, but Mamma Mia 2 isn't actually set

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.850
now, which means that tech-wise, the Mamma
Mia universe is well ahead of its time!

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.440
OK, I'm going to run through Mamma Mia's timeline
to show you what I mean.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.320
When Mamma Mia was released in 2008, the date
the story was set in was rather vague, with

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.900
little to tie it down to a particular year.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.190
But Here We Go Again changed that, giving
us with some specific dates for movie detectives

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.190
to create a timeline of the events in both
films.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:18.659
Lily James' prequel story reveals that Donna
graduated in 1979, after which she hooked

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:23.360
up with Sophie's three potential fathers during
the summer after graduation, which lines up

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.810
roughly with the months mentioned in Donna's
diary in the first film.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:32.229
So, Donna became pregnant with Sophie during
the summer of 1979 which, allowing nine months

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:35.260
for the pregnancy, means Sophie was born in
1980.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.220
We know that in the first film Sophie is 20
years old.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.240
"If she were more like me, she wouldn't be
getting married at 20."

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.750
So, given Sophie was born in 1980, the first
film must have been set in the year 2000.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:52.610
As for Here We Go Again, that's set five years
after the original movie in 2005.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:56.330
We know that because when Ruby says she wants
to "commit to being a grandmother", Sophie

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:59.500
accuses her of being 25 years too late.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.750
And Amanda Seyfried confirmed this five-year
gap between the stories during press for the

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:04.750
film.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:08.060
The problem with the tech in Mamma Mia 2 is
that it doesn't match this timeline.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.980
The first iPhones weren't released until 2007,
and the characters in the movie are actually

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.209
using pretty up-to-date ones, with Sophie's
iPhone looking like it's probably a model

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:20.010
from around 2013 onwards.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.920
While the 2-in-1 laptop-tablet used by the
execs in Harry's meeting is from a range that

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.660
was only introduced in 2013.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.110
All of which means that while the Greek island
of Kalokairi isn't exactly Europe's answer

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.519
to Wakanda, somehow they've still managed
to nab themselves some hi-tech gadgets that

00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:40.190
weren't even available at the time the movie's
set!

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:45.109
Another timeline problem pops up with Donna's
graduation in 1979 at New College, Oxford

00:02:45.109 --> 00:02:50.549
where, according to Lily James, the idea was
that Donna was the first ever female valedictorian.

00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:55.639
But while this scene certainly gets 10 out
of 10 for fun, it gets far fewer points for

00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:56.639
accuracy.

00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:00.650
The reason being that, while New College,
Oxford was founded in 1379, they didn't admit

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:04.449
the first female undergraduates until 1979.

00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:08.719
And as Oxford's academic year starts in October,
it would have been impossible for Donna to

00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:13.739
graduate in the summer of 1979 as that would
have been before any women undergraduates

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:16.019
had ever been admitted to New College.

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:20.139
On top of that, valedictorian speeches and
honours are typically a North American tradition

00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:22.890
and not something that happened previously
at Oxford.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.150
I think the filmmakers decided to go with
a valedictorian speech for Donna as they needed

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.409
an excuse for her to get up on stage and also
wanted to show her as a trailblazer and the

00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:33.959
kind of person who shattered conventions.

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:38.469
Writer-director Ol Parker told EW that to
do that they needed to put Donna "in the stuffiest

00:03:38.469 --> 00:03:41.540
possible surrounding and then have her shake
that up."

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.859
Parker chose Oxford as he says it was "the
most fabulously pompous place [they] could

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:46.859
find.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:47.859
[In a good way.]

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:48.859
Fabulously learned and impressive.

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:53.120
[So,] It was all the funnier for Lily to rip
off her costume and go for it."

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.650
And Donna's age when she graduates in 1979
creates another issue with the timeline as

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.829
she was probably several years too young.

00:03:59.829 --> 00:04:04.790
Andy Garcia, who plays Fernando Cienfuegos,
has said his character could well be Donna's

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:09.159
father and the movie seems to imply it was
Fernando's love affair with Ruby which led

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:11.420
to Ruby becoming pregnant with Donna.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.920
In the film, Cienfuegos reveals that his romance
with Ruby took place in 1959 and, assuming

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:22.340
that's when Donna was conceived, she would
have been born in late 1959 or 1960.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.650
That would make Donna around 18 to 19 years
old at the time of her graduation in summer

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.340
1979.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.130
As undergraduate courses in Oxford are typically
three to four years long, Donna must have

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.920
started college when she was only around 15
or so years old.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.340
Now, it's not unheard of for child prodigies
to go to college at a young age, but as much

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.830
as we all love Donna, that's never been part
of her story.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:48.680
Also, as absolutely loads of you have pointed
out, when it comes to Donna's best friends,

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:53.680
Tanya and Rosie, the timeline is rather fuzzy
about who knew who, what and when.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.180
In the first film, it's clearly news to Tanya
and Rosie that anyone other than Sam could

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.210
have been Sophie's dad.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.080
"Remember how I said it was Sam?"

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.230
"Sam the architect, who had to go home to
get married?"

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:12.570
"I'm not sure that it was him, because there
were two other guys around the same time."

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:13.570
"Donna Sheridan.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:14.570
You shady lady."

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.600
"Why didn't you tell us?"

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.870
And when Tanya and Rosie see Bill in the original
film, we don't get any sense they've ever

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:23.870
met before.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:28.180
Yet in Here We Go Again, we learn they already
met Bill when they were young and they even

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.640
encouraged Donna to get over Sam with him.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.990
What's more, Rosie even had a big crush on
young Bill!

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:37.840
All of which sounds like the kind of things
you might remember when they crop up later!

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.290
But maybe all that Greek sun and ouzo simply
went to their heads!

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:45.860
And there's also the puzzling case of Harry's
missing guitar and switched around diary dates,

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:49.900
and if you'd like to know more about that,
check out my video in the card here or video

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.720
description below.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.590
We already know that technology can travel
back from the future in the Mamma Mia universe,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:06:01.000
so it shouldn't be a surprise that human beings
can also time travel in this absolutely fabulous

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.000
world!

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.350
Thanks to eagle-eyed Twitter user Tom Zohar,
we now know that this dancing waiter travelled

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:12.300
from 1979 to 2005 without ever looking a single
year older, though he did have time for a

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.530
quick shave!

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.680
The dancer in question is Justin Thomas who
showed up in both the Waterloo routine in

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.470
Paris with young Donna and Harry and then
again over 25 years later when Cher made her

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:24.370
grand entrance on the island.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.410
So, either Cher really can turn back time
or the dancer has a portrait of himself hidden

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:31.460
away like Dorian Gray.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.530
And here's a little bonus timeline craziness
for you.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:39.310
Given the sequel is set in 2005, there's no
way that the grand reopening party for the

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:44.310
Hotel Bella Donna could have been on Saturday
July 7th, which is the date we see on Sophie's

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.780
invites, because, in 2005, July 7th was a
Thursday!

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.130
Now, obviously, when they shot the first film,
they never planned on making another one,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:57.420
so they didn't worry about how story decisions
they made might impact a sequel.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.990
And as events in the Mamma Mia! stage musical
took place in 1999, the year the show opened

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.860
in the West End, that's likely where the filmmakers
got inspiration for their timeline.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:10.150
For me, there's far too much fun to be had
watching both the Mamma Mia movies for these

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.440
timeline issues to get in the way.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.990
After all, the Mamma-Mia-verse is all about
the joy and the music!

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:20.401
So, as Cher says in the sequel, 'let's concentrate
on the here and now."

00:07:20.401 --> 00:07:24.120
And, on that note, if you'd like to check
out 10 amazing details in Mamma Mia 2 that

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.840
will make you love the sequel even more, then
tap on the card or the link in the video description

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:30.300
or at the end of this video.

